<h1>Video 083 — Ways of defining a Thread Part-2</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Multi Threading Part-3 || The ways of defining a Thread Part-2</p>
<p>Source: real YouTube auto-captions (.transcripts/083-8xnNgR0zeS8.txt). ASR: runable / renable = Runnable; keep subing = keep copying; threat = thread; target runable = target Runnable; pan Kalan / P Kalan = his demo customized name (e.g. “Pavan Kalyan”); thread I zero / thread iphone zero / thread ion zero = Thread-0; Artic exception = ArithmeticException; Dura’s / duras approach = Durga’s approach; SCJP = OCJP/SCJP.</p>
<h3>00:08 — Heading: defining a thread by implementing Runnable</h3>
<p>Second approach. Diagram vs first approach:</p>
<ul><li>First: MyThread extends Thread, and Thread already implements Runnable.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Second: implement Runnable directly — MyRunnable implements Runnable — without extending Thread.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>// First approach (reminder)
class MyThread extends Thread { /* ... */ }

// Second approach
class MyRunnable implements Runnable { /* ... */ }</pre>
<h3>03:47 — Runnable interface facts (board)</h3>
<ul><li>Present in `java.lang` package.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Contains only one method: run.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>// java.lang.Runnable
public void run();</pre>
<h3>05:07 — Full second-approach example</h3>
<pre class='code'>class MyRunnable implements Runnable {
    public void run() {
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
            System.out.println(&quot;Child Thread&quot;);
        }
    }
}

class ThreadDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyRunnable r = new MyRunnable();   // main creates MyRunnable object
        Thread t = new Thread(r);          // r = target Runnable
        t.start();
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
            System.out.println(&quot;Main Thread&quot;);
        }
    }
}
// mixed output — we can&#x27;t tell exact output</pre>
<p>Only one-word difference from approach 1: `implements Runnable` instead of `extends Thread`. Code inside run = job of the thread. Total writing of this class = defining a thread.</p>
<p>Starting steps (main thread still only one until start):</p>
<ol><li>Create MyRunnable object — “car is ready.”</li></ol>
<ol><li>MyRunnable has no start (Runnable interface also has no start) — need someone with start capability → Thread.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Thread t = new Thread(r); then t.start();.</li></ol>
<p>Without passing r:</p>
<p>Thread t = new Thread(); // no target
t.start();
// Thread class run() (empty) — not MyRunnable.run()</p>
<p>With r passed, Thread’s start ends up calling MyRunnable’s `run`. That object is the target Runnable.</p>
<p>After t.start(): two threads — main runs remaining loop; child runs run.</p>
<p>Student digression about starting another thread / restart: restarting same thread → IllegalThreadStateException (from Part-2).</p>
<h3>16:32 — Case study: with vs without Runnable argument</h3>
<p>MyRunnable r = new MyRunnable();
Thread t1 = new Thread();    // without r
Thread t2 = new Thread(r);   // with r</p>
<h4>Case 1 — `t1.start()`</h4>
<p>t1.start();</p>
<ul><li>New thread will be created.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Executes Thread class `run` (empty implementation) → essentially no useful output from job.</li></ul>
<h4>Case 2 — `t1.run()`</h4>
<p>t1.run();</p>
<ul><li>No new thread.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Thread class run executed like a normal method call.</li></ul>
<h4>Case 3 — `t2.start()`</h4>
<p>t2.start();</p>
<ul><li>New thread will be created.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Executes MyRunnable `run` — proper way.</li></ul>
<h4>Case 4 — `t2.run()`</h4>
<p>t2.run();</p>
<ul><li>No new thread.</li></ul>
<ul><li>MyRunnable run executed like a normal method call.</li></ul>
<h4>Case 5 — `r.start()`</h4>
<p>r.start();
// CE: cannot find symbol
// symbol: method start()
// location: class MyRunnable</p>
<p>MyRunnable has no start. If Runnable had start, no need to create Thread object.</p>
<h4>Case 6 — `r.run()`</h4>
<p>r.run();</p>
<ul><li>No new thread.</li></ul>
<ul><li>MyRunnable run like a normal method call.</li></ul>
<h3>30:11 — Which approach is best? (interview)</h3>
<p>Two ways to define a thread. Which is recommended?</p>
<p>Implementing Runnable is recommended over extending Thread.</p>
<p>Reason he wants you to recite:</p>
<ul><li>First approach: class always extends Thread → no chance of extending any other class → missing inheritance benefit.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Second approach: while implementing Runnable you can still extend another class → won’t miss inheritance benefit.</li></ul>
<p>Also: memory / performance level reasons may exist internally; conclusion stays — implements Runnable approach is recommended than extending Thread.</p>
<pre class='code'>// First approach — inheritance already &quot;used up&quot; on Thread
class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() { /* job */ }
}

// Second — can still extend something else
class MyRunnable extends SomeOtherClass implements Runnable {
    public void run() { /* job */ }
}</pre>
<h3>38:24 — Thread class constructors (eight)</h3>
<p>He lists constructors present in Thread class (whether you use all or not):</p>
<pre class='code'>Thread t = new Thread();

Thread t = new Thread(Runnable r);

Thread t = new Thread(String name);

Thread t = new Thread(Runnable r, String name);

Thread t = new Thread(ThreadGroup g, String name);

Thread t = new Thread(ThreadGroup g, Runnable r);

Thread t = new Thread(ThreadGroup g, Runnable r, String name);

Thread t = new Thread(ThreadGroup g, Runnable r, String name, long stackSize);</pre>
<p>Notes as he speaks:</p>
<ul><li>Every thread has a name.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Every thread belongs to some ThreadGroup (group by functionality).</li></ul>
<ul><li>For every thread JVM creates a runtime stack; last constructor lets you specify stack size.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Total: eight constructors.</li></ul>
<h3>45:49 — Durga’s approach (hybrid — not recommended, but valid)</h3>
<p>Heading: Durga’s approach to define a thread (not recommended to use)</p>
<p>Define with first approach (extends Thread) but start like second approach (wrap in new Thread(target)):</p>
<pre class='code'>class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Child Thread&quot;);
    }
}

class ThreadDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        Thread t1 = new Thread(t); // t is acceptable as Runnable (Thread implements Runnable)
        t1.start();
        System.out.println(&quot;Main Thread&quot;);
    }
}
// possible: Child Thread then Main Thread, or Main Thread then Child Thread</pre>
<p>Why valid: MyThread extends Thread, Thread implements Runnable → MyThread is-a Runnable object → can pass as target.</p>
<p>Hybrid: definition style of approach 1, starting style of approach 2. In SCJP/OCJP if you see this, do not mark invalid — it is valid. Still not recommended as a teaching “standard” approach.</p>
<h3>51:19 — Getting and setting name of a thread</h3>
<p>Every thread in Java has some name — default name generated by JVM, or customized name provided by programmer.</p>
<pre class='code'>class MyThread extends Thread {
    // run optional for analyzing name properties
}

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName());
        // prints main

        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        System.out.println(t.getName());
        // prints Thread-0

        // MyThread t2 = new MyThread();
        // System.out.println(t2.getName()); // Thread-1

        Thread.currentThread().setName(&quot;Pavan Kalyan&quot;);
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName());
        // prints Pavan Kalyan

        // optional demo he runs:
        // System.out.println(10 / 0);
        // Exception in thread &quot;Pavan Kalyan&quot; java.lang.ArithmeticException: / by zero
        // (name &quot;main&quot; replaced)
    }
}</pre>
<p>Board theory:</p>
<ul><li>Every thread in Java has some name — default name generated by JVM or customized name provided by programmer.</li></ul>
<ul><li>We can get and set name by:</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>public final String getName()
public final void setName(String name)</pre>
<p>Default child names: first child Thread-0, next Thread-1, …</p>
<p>Even main thread’s name can be changed happily.</p>
<h3>1:08:25 — `Thread.currentThread()` — current executing thread object</h3>
<p>Static method on Thread: Thread.currentThread() returns the currently executing thread object.</p>
<pre class='code'>class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println(
            &quot;run method executed by the thread &quot;
                + Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }
}

class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        t.start();
        System.out.println(
            &quot;main method executed by the thread &quot;
                + Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }
}
// possible mixed order of the two lines
// prints ... main method ... main
// prints ... run method ... Thread-0</pre>
<ul><li>Inside main: currentThread() → main thread.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Inside run (after proper start): currentThread() → child (e.g. Thread-0).</li></ul>
<p>Board note: We can get current executing thread object by using Thread.currentThread() method (static method of Thread class).</p>
<h3>1:18:31 — Close of Part-3</h3>
<p>How to get/set name of a thread and how to get current executing thread object reference — clear. Series continues with priorities next.</p>
